{
  "dom_id": "step-q110863",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Let $D=[1,2]\\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2-xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{8},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2y}{8},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$",
  "worked_solution": "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\nf(2,2)=2-2\\cdot 2=-2≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $g(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2x+y}{8}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2x+y}{8}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy}{8}]}_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y+3}{8}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+3y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h$  is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2y}{8}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2y}{8}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2xy]}_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}(2y+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[{y}^{2}+\\frac{3y}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8} \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
